---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import Corridor from '../components/Corridor.astro'
import {loadDepartments} from '../lib/ministry'

export const prerender = false
const departments = await loadDepartments()
---

<Layout title="How it works" wide>
  <div class="head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Internal circular · Not for citizens · Citizens may read it anyway</p>
    <h1>How the Ministry works</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The process is not code. It is a workflow definition stored in Sanity, next to the submissions it acts on. Every run
      is a document too: which stage it is in, what the clerks said, who pressed what, and when. The public site only
      reads the workflow back.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Corridor departments={departments} currentStage="department-b" visits={[{name: 'intake'}, {name: 'department-a'}]} />

  <div class="cols">
    <section class="sheet">
      <p class="eyebrow">The corridor</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>Front Desk.</strong> Your form is stamped in automatically. If a department sends it back, this is where you answer.</li>
        <li><strong>Department of Pedantry.</strong> An AI clerk reads your form and finds the one thing missing. It cites a regulation it made up. Answer it and it accepts, grudgingly.</li>
        <li><strong>Department of Rubber Stamps.</strong> An AI clerk approves everything and says something that proves it did not read the form.</li>
        <li><strong>The Archive.</strong> An AI archivist files your form. Every third serial is lost, once. You get a receipt and go back to the Front Desk.</li>
        <li><strong>The Minister's Desk.</strong> A human. Approves with a note, rejects with a reason, or refers the file back to a department. One hour to rule, or the Department of Delays sends the file back to the Archive, which loses it.</li>
        <li><strong>Office of Certificates.</strong> A certificate document is written with the clerks' remarks as stamps, rendered to an image, and a Discord webhook announces it.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="sheet">
      <p class="eyebrow">What is Sanity here</p>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li>Submissions, departments, clerks, certificates and receipts are documents in dataset <code>ministry</code> of project <code>v6745vem</code>.</li>
        <li>The clerks' personalities are content: a clerk document holds the system prompt. Slide the temperament in Studio and the prompt rewrites itself. Their portraits are generated by Agent Actions from that prompt.</li>
        <li>The corridor is a Sanity Workflows definition. Each department is a stage; each AI clerk is an effect; each human decision is an action. Both move the run through the same transitions. Guards freeze the form while a department holds it.</li>
        <li>Effects run in a Sanity Function that wakes when a run gains unclaimed work. The clerks call Agent Actions (Prompt) with the form as a GROQ parameter. A second Function ticks the clock once an hour for the Department of Delays.</li>
        <li>The Minister's back office is an App SDK app in the Sanity Dashboard: the corridor as a live board, one file open at a time, a workflow diagram, and the same actions the CLI can fire.</li>
        <li>The definition has a test suite that runs the real engine in memory: the lost loop, the delays, the guards, all without a network.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="small muted type">Built for the Sanity Challenge on DEV. The code is public; the link is in the post.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .head {
    max-width: 780px;
    margin-bottom: 22px;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 17px;
  }
  .cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 28px;
    margin-top: 30px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .steps li,
  .facts li {
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .steps strong {
    font-family: var(--serif);
  }
  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .cols {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
